 ### Sparse Ray Marching for RTAO
-A variant of sparse ray marching used for Ray Traced Ambient Occlusion. It marches from a surface point in a hemisphere direction, counting occlusions up to a radius limit [S5, unofficial]. It starts at the floor of the ray origin, initializes local position and distance to 0, and loops up to $3 \times \text{uniforms.grid\_ext}$ times [S5, unofficial]. It checks voxel occupancy using `voxel_masks`; if occupied, it returns true (occlusion found) [S5, unofficial]. It loads the voxel AADF and calculates the exit plane, then calculates side distance and $t$, adding $t$ to the accumulated distance [S5, unofficial]. If distance $> t_{max}$, it returns false (no occlusion found within range) [S5, unofficial]. It advances the ray position, updating `ipos` and `local_pos` [S5, unofficial]. Unlike the main sparse ray march, this version does not use the hierarchical chunk/brick structure. It only checks `voxel_masks` and `voxel_aadf`. It also accumulates distance and stops at $t_{max}$ [S5, unofficial]. Parameter: $t_{max}$ (maximum distance to march) [S5, unofficial].
+A variant of sparse ray marching used for Ray Traced Ambient Occlusion [S6, unofficial]. It marches from a surface point in a hemisphere direction, counting occlusions up to a radius limit [S6, unofficial]. It starts at the floor of the ray origin, initializes local position and distance to 0, and loops up to $3 \times \text{uniforms.grid\_ext}$ times [S6, unofficial]. It checks voxel occupancy using `voxel_masks`; if occupied, it returns true (occlusion found) [S6, unofficial]. It loads the voxel AADF and calculates the exit plane, then calculates side distance and $t$, adding $t$ to the accumulated distance [S6, unofficial]. If distance $> t_{max}$, it returns false (no occlusion found within range) [S6, unofficial]. It advances the ray position, updating `ipos` and `local_pos` [S6, unofficial]. Unlike the main sparse ray march, this version does not use the hierarchical chunk/brick structure [S6, unofficial]. It only checks `voxel_masks` and `voxel_aadf` [S6, unofficial]. It also accumulates distance and stops at $t_{max}$ [S6, unofficial]. Parameter: $t_{max}$ (maximum distance to march) [S6, unofficial].
 
 ### Robust Interval Arithmetic Ray Traversal
-A ray traversal method that uses interval arithmetic with correct rounding modes to guarantee that no octant is missed during traversal, solving precision errors that occur when subdivision levels approach floating-point limits [S6]. It performs ray traversal using interval arithmetic with correct rounding modes (using SSE2 instructions) to determine the order of sub-octants [S6]. Precision is 30 bits (NL=30), with resolution at Octree level 9 (512^3 resolution) [S6]. When subdivision levels approach the limits of floating-point precision, standard traversals mismatch. This is handled by using 128-bit precision binary floating-point arithmetic for verification and interval arithmetic with correct rounding modes for the actual algorithm [S6]. Unlike Gargantini's method which only partially eliminates errors and requires restricted conditions, this method guarantees correctness for all cases with minimal performance impact [S6].
